  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 230 PM CDT Wed Sep 2 2026\n\nAfter this morning's showers, conditions remain fairly calm over the \nforecast area this afternoon. The main item of note for the \nremainder of the day today are strong winds gusting up to roughly 25 \nmiles per hour, mainly along and west of the Missouri River. \nDespite the winds, humidity will remain high enough to keep \nGrassland Fire Danger at the Moderate category or lower for the \nremainder of the afternoon. \n\nMain forecast focus today is the severe threat tomorrow evening \nnorth of a warm front moving into the area. Storms are expected to \ndevelop mainly over North Dakota in the late afternoon to early \nevening time frame, with the possibility of some development across \nthe border into northern South Dakota. One of the main potential \nlimiting factors for storms developing this far south will be a \nminor capping inversion showing up in some model soundings (likely a \nresult of the frontal boundary to the south). 850mb temperatures are \nset to reach 22-25 Celsius over northeastern South Dakota and \nwestern Minnesota, with an inversion underneath. At this point in \nthe forecast cycle, hi-res guidance is fairly split on whether or \nnot storms will be able to develop or not. Should storms develop in \nthat area, ample MUCAPE upwards of 2000 J/kg will be present along \nwith shear of roughly 40 knots and strong mid-level lapse rates of 7-\n8 C/km all working to sustain a hail threat. Wind will also be a \npotential threat. Widespread DCAPE > 1000 J/kg will be present, and \nthere are also hints of a linear storm mode in some of the models \nthat do resolve convection this far south, which would also aid in \nthe potential wind threat. At this point confidence in the severe \nthreat is still somewhat low due to the lack of model consistency, \nbut the necessary ingredients will certainly be in place. \n\nThrough at least the weekend, the broad upper-level setup of a \ntrough to the west and a ridge over the central CONUS is expected to \nhold, with the ridge deepening over the next couple of days. As a \nresult of the subsequent height rises over the region, above normal \ntemperatures (highs in the 90s through Saturday) will take hold. \nThis setup will also place the Northern Plains in a position such \nthat the trough will be able to eject jet streaks and shortwaves \ninto the region during the weekend and beyond. The divergence aloft \nover the area will support the next chances for showers and storms, \nover an isolated area on Saturday and becoming more widespread on \nSunday. Mid-range guidance does highlight the potential for a severe \nthreat, with probabilities at this point lining up with a Marginal \nRisk Outlook. Additional chances for showers and storms come Monday \ninto Tuesday as the upper-level low driving the trough is set to \nprogress to the northeast and brings stronger divergence aloft over \nthe Northern Plains.",
